tent well we're kind of learning  what's happening with that at the moment  because we don't have much wind right  now and at the same time we're seeing  something like this this this shows you  uh gas prices it's a future contract for  the future month uh going back a year  and just look at how much they've risen  in that period up by  450 to the highest level uh on record  there's all sorts of reasons for it and  partly it comes down to that the fact  that we're reliant more on it because  the wind isn't blowing as strongly but  you've also got less gas coming in from  russia you've got some  oil fields and gas fields which are shut  down for maintenance at the moment  you've got fewer stockpiles of gas right  now  but that is having a consequence right  now and we're seeing some companies  shutting down their production as a  result of that  and another thing that we have certainly  noticed recently is when you look at  electricity prices so specifically  electricity prices there was a fire in  an interconnector that brings some of  that electricity from france to the uk  uh just in just in the past week and  look at what that's done to electricity  prices  it really just reinforces the fact that  when we're talking about our energy  policy you can't just talk about  renewables you can't just talk about  wind turbines and solar you've got to  talk about what you do as a backup if  they're not bringing them much power  so as we're hearing from ed there  there's been changes to the uk energy  supply because of a number of factors we  want to bring in our data dashboard here  because it shows you the real time power  mix for electricity fossil fuels are  coming in at 53  at the moment renewables at 31 and  nuclear at 16 it's important to remember  though that that power mix will vary  according to the time of year and even  the time of day a national grid who we  spoke to today is keen for us to put  that into context  now prince william says that he launched  the earth shot prize so that he could  look his children in the eye knowing  that he had done his bit for climate  change the environmental prize  recognizes those who are coming up with  creative solutions to tackle the issue  and this year's finalists show that  there is no mission too big or indeed  too small when it comes to climate  action as sky's royal correspondent  rhiannon mills reports  a global prize designed to motivate and  inspire a new generation of thinkers  it's prince william's biggest ever  project the earthshot prize injecting  optimism into the climate debate  the ambition quality and range of  submissions has been amazing and should  fit us all with optimism and hope that  our goals for this decisive decade are  achievable  the 15 finalists include conservation  projects the city of milan and the  republic of costa rica and  groundbreaking tech innovation vinisha  umashanka from india is 14 and has  designed a solar powered ironing cart  in uk most of you are in at home right  with an electrical iron box  now here instead of ironing at home  most of us have to go to a shop like a  small stall where ironing vendors use  charcoal based iron boxes each of them  burn five kilos of charcoal every day  so that's 50 million kg of chocolate  every day in india alone knowing that  it's quite unsustainable i decided to  keep the same vibe and tradition but  make it more sustainable and modern that  it suits today's needs and our present  generation  william has been working with figures  like david attenborough convinced too  much pessimism was making people switch  off the five winners of the one million  pound prizes will be announced on the  17th of october the beginning of a  decade-long defining project for a  future king rhiannon mills sky news  hello again and welcome back to the  daily climate show here on sky news  we're getting straight into discussing  the climate issues of the day that's  with the honorary president of the black  environment network judy linguang and  business greens editor-in-chief james  murray good evening to you both good to  see you well we're going to start with  those protests on the m25 the country's  busiest motorway again blocked in  various places for the third time this  week by protesters a group called  insulate britain does this kind of  action make a difference or is it  counterproductive to their cause i  should say actually in the last hour  we've heard from the transport secretary  grant shaps who said that we all want to  live in a world that's cleaner but this  is counterproductive this winds up  motorists safety is put at risk and of  course traffic standing is the worst  thing for pollution so let's get  straight in there  judy what do you think and pretty patel  says these are selfish guerrilla  tacticians what do you reckon  well you know of course extinction  rebellion does inconvenience and upset  some people but extinction rebellion has  leaders are very clued up about the fact  that the design of a protest is about  getting media and therefore public  attention in order to get the message to  the highest number of people from my  experience once we will see the message  you know they then center on the message  what do you think about this james i  mean do you think extinction rebellion  have achieved their goals  well if their primary goal is to get  attention and get this uh issue in the  public eye and up the agenda then they  absolutely have by definition  i mean what i always think is most  interesting in some ways about these  protests is  that how inevitable they feel i mean if  you accept that we're in the in the  grips of a climate crisis that's getting  worse and worse and could have very very  grave repercussions for humanity as a  whole over the coming decades then it  was always the case that a group of  group within society was going to be  willing to take direct action to try and  resolve  that challenge and ensure that humanity  as a whole takes 