e critiques of extinction  rebellion from the off was that it was  too broad brush in some ways too  ambitious asking to get to net zero  emissions by 2030 is something that you  know isn't going to happen that that's  too big a lift but but what's  interesting about this is we are now  starting to see these more specific asks  and the idea of um having a national  program to insulate and improve the  energy efficiency of our building stock  i mean that's supported by the committee  on climate change that's supported by  the cbi that's supported by virtually  every think tank and green group going  that works in this space it's it's an  investment  that would deliver massive economic  benefits it would improve health it  would cut energy bills and it would cut  carbon emissions and it would help  resolve some of these um energy issues  you were just talking about with energy  price spikes because we'd be using less  energy in the first place so you know it  is a very um can he ask and it's  something that the government really  should be considering and prioritizing  if it wants to be taken seriously as a  global climate leader and you you never  you never um no we we might even get to  see the heat and building strategy  before cop 26 been long awaited hasn't  it well let's move on now to the search  for solutions we were hearing earlier in  the show about the earthshop prize it is  of course prince william's campaign to  find solutions and innovation to tackle  climate change from across the world  around half of those finalists announced  today were from the global south well  what does that say about our search for  solutions and judy there's a real  simplicity to some of these um  shortlisted uh solutions isn't there i  mean costa rica making micro payments to  people to just do their bit to protect a  little bit of rainforest  absolutely you know there are things  that only governments can do and that's  where all the protests and pressure  comes on but indeed there's some things  that individuals sections of society  like business and  ngos and so on can do and they do it  well because they're in touch with very  local characteristics and cultural  dimensions you know take the case of 14  year old phoenicia she notices that in  her country millions of people still use  street ironing services and she's  created a solar-powered ironing card in  a very sunny country to make a  difference it is amazing and you know  and i applaud prince william for saying  that he needs to be able to look his  children in the eye and say he did this  bit  you know as much as we live in a  science-dominated world it's still our  emotions and our commitment to family  that drives our strongest motivation to  act and to be fascinated inspired by  people that look like us  having amazing though it's important to  note though james isn't it that there  are no british short listies on that  list many of the ideas that were  shortlisted from from the global south  are we a bit arrogant in thinking that  it's g7 western nations that really have  the answers here  um i think there's a risk of that i  think there's a risk of in in every part  of the world is that countries see the  climate crisis and the sort of the clean  tech revolution that will hopefully help  resolve it as these kind of national  local regional issues but that's true  everywhere and and one of the most  exciting things about some of the  stories we cover at business green is  the huge breadth of of innovation and  investment that we're seeing in all  parts of the world including very much  in these emerging markets and in the  developing world i mean there's one of  these talking points you get a lot from  people who are opposed to climate action  you say well what about china what about  india what about these big polluting  countries that are you know are  investing in coal yes they are but  they're also in china's case the world's  biggest renewables market the world's  biggest electric vehicle market and we  are seeing um some catalyzing progress  uh from some of these countries that are  in some ways because they don't have the  legacy high carbon infrastructure  they're able to leapfrog that and deploy  clean technologies at scale at pace uh  ahead of some industrialized nation so  it's much more simplistic than the idea  that the industrialized world is going  to solve this on its own and then kind  of through its large s  help developing countries i think that  you know that is a borderline  neo-colonialist way to see it necessity  is often the mother of invention isn't  it it's been really good to talk to you  both tonight judy ling wong and james  murray thank you

